            <ArticleTitle>TIMI Risk Score predicts early readmission.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>324-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess if the TIMI Risk Score could predict early readmission.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">869 consecutive admissions to a Scottish district general hospital with suspected acute coronary syndrome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A computerised clinical information system was interrogated to verify readmission. Area under the receiver operator characteristic curve and chi-square test for trend between TIMI Risk Score and readmission rate were calculated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Median follow up was 73 days. There was a strong association between TIMI Risk Score and readmission rate (chi-square test for trend, p&lt;0.001), with an area under the receiver operator characteristic curve of 0.60 (95% C.I. 0.55-0.65).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The TIMI Risk Score can predict readmission. This study reinforces its utility as a tool for identifying patients more likely to benefit from aggressive intervention.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
